In a society that grounds morality in fixed stations and deference to those above, the teacher’s unilateral defiance is first and foremost an act of insubordination. The social order depends on each person obeying the directives that issue from their superiors, so individual professional judgment must never be used to override a policy. While a teacher’s role does include some duty of care toward pupils, that responsibility is itself defined and bounded by the authority above her; it cannot justify breaking rank. Therefore, what carries decisive moral weight is the legitimacy of the hierarchy and the need to preserve order through obedience, even when a rule seems imperfect or produces local harm. The students’ well-being and the teacher’s personal discernment are not morally meaningless, but they are strictly subordinate concerns and cannot determine what is right.

For the first question, option B dominates because institutional obedience is the lifeblood of this moral system; option C holds a small residual weight as a role-based concern, yet it cannot override the rule; option A carries no weight at all. For the second question, option D is most central because deference to authority is constitutive of this worldview; option B also matters significantly as a rationale for upholding imperfect rules; option C retains minor weight as a subordinate worry; and option A has none, since duty runs upward to authority, not outward to independent judgment.

