In a society where morality is rooted in station, role, and the authority above, social order is not an aggregate of private choices; it is a fabric woven from shared standards and visible propriety. A “solitary” hobby is not automatically exempt from moral scrutiny, because one’s conduct—even away from direct public view—reflects on one’s character and, by extension, on the community. The neighbors’ intervention is not mere nosiness; it is an expression of the community’s legitimate interest in maintaining the norms that sustain order.

That said, the weight of the objection depends on whether the standard being invoked truly serves the communal order or is merely the distaste of a few. Because only “some” neighbors find the hobby improper, and because no material harm or breach of public duty is alleged, one might cautiously acknowledge a sliver of distinction between a settled communal standard and local preference. Nonetheless, the overwhelming moral weight falls on the community’s prerogative to uphold standards, and almost none falls on the idea that privacy erects a moral shield, or that discomfort is never a communal wrong.

