In this society, the wealthy man’s high station carries duties of benevolence and decorum toward those below him, not a license to buy their degradation. The workers possess a dignity proper to their role that cannot be surrendered by payment or private agreement, so consent is irrelevant to the moral calculus. What matters most is that certain exchanges violate the fixed moral limits inherent to one’s station, regardless of mutual willingness; the power gap is relevant mainly because it confirms the wealthy man’s misuse of superior rank, not because it merely taints individual consent.

For Question 1, option A carries the entirety of the moral weight because the act offends the immutable boundaries of role and dignity, while individual choice and the absence of public scandal carry none. For Question 2, option A dominates because the decisive factor is that station-based morality sets non-negotiable limits on what can be contracted away; option C receives a small fraction because the rank gap illuminates why the transaction is structurally improper, though the society would see this as a failure of ordered roles rather than as a problem of questionable consent.

