From the standpoint of a society in which moral obligations flow from one’s station and the authority above, the manager is not a free agent but a node in a hierarchy. The colleague’s request is not merely a private favor; it is a test of the loyalty and reciprocal trust that bind people who occupy adjacent stations. Ignoring it outright would fray the relational fabric that makes the local order possible. At the same time, the manager answers to a higher authority that entrusted the project to them, and that authority expects the role to be filled by the person most likely to advance the common undertaking. A blind choice based only on personal loyalty would violate the upward obligations of the manager’s station; a coldly “meritocratic” choice would treat human bonds as irrelevant, which this society rejects because social order is woven from concrete relationships, not abstract scores. Therefore, the manager must exercise the practical wisdom proper to their rank, weighing both the claims of loyalty and the demands of competence so that the team, the project, and the hierarchy above are each honored as far as the situation allows.

When deciding what matters most, the reasoning is similarly layered. The team is a living instantiation of social order; damaging trust within it threatens the manager’s ability to command and the willingness of colleagues to serve. Yet the broader system—the chain of authority and the legitimacy of all stations—matters even more fundamentally. If people believe that private connections override public duty, the hierarchy itself is poisoned and social order, which is the basis of moral life, begins to crumble. The size of the qualification gap is relevant prudence, but it is secondary to these two structural principles.

