In a society governed by rank and role, the eldest son’s station fixes his primary obligation to his parents and the lineage; the family workshop is not mere personal property but a public trust sustaining social order across generations. While a father certainly owes his children nurture and a husband owes his wife protection, these duties are hierarchically nested within—rather than equal to—the higher authority of filial piety and ancestral continuity, so the pull of the immediate family is felt but cannot override his station. The very idea that an individual must personally invent the answer is foreign to this worldview, because moral legitimacy flows from tradition and the authority above, not from private judgment, so tradition itself is what resolves the dilemma.

